<BaseLayout title="Documentation" page="docs"><div class="page-wrap"><header class="page-intro"><span class="eyebrow">FIELD NOTES / DOCUMENTATION</span><h1>Your route into Zusal.</h1><p>Understand the boundaries, connect the prototype, and know what’s ready.</p></header><div class="docs-layout"><nav class="docs-nav" aria-label="Documentation sections"><a href="#quickstart">Quickstart</a><a href="#how-it-works">How routing works</a><a href="#compatibility">Compatibility</a><a href="#account">Account & devices</a><a href="#usage">Understanding usage</a><a href="#privacy">Data & privacy</a><a href="#support">Troubleshooting</a></nav><div class="docs-body"><section id="quickstart"><h2>Start with the Pi source prototype.</h2><p>The current router is a Pi extension plus a local selection backend. Follow the source project README for configuration; don't paste provider keys into this website or Discord.</p><ol><li>Install Node.js 22.19+ and the supported Pi version.</li><li>In a trusted <code>pi-router</code> checkout, copy <code>.env.example</code> to your local <code>.env</code> and configure the backend. Keep secrets outside version control.</li><li>Make the server catalog overlap with models actually authenticated and allowed in Pi.</li><li>Start the backend with <code>npm start</code>; in another terminal run <code>npm run pi</code>.</li><li>Select <code>/model our-router/auto</code> inside Pi.</li></ol><p>Plain <code>pi</code> does not automatically load the local extension. A health check proves liveness, not successful Jev or provider authentication.</p><a href="/download">Platform instructions ↗</a></section><section id="how-it-works"><h2>Selection is our job.<br />Execution stays with the harness.</h2><p>The plugin sends compact routing context and eligible model identities to the selection backend. Jev assesses the request and helps select a model. The plugin validates that choice and returns it to Pi’s native virtual-model callback.</p><p>Pi handles provider credentials, model calls, streaming, tools, retries, and conversation state. Zusal is not another agent harness or a provider-key vault.</p><p>Continuations within a tool-using turn retain an eligible route. A new user turn can select again. Current policy and catalog evidence live in the router source; no measured task-quality or savings guarantee is claimed.</p></section><section id="compatibility"><h2>Compatibility, without guesswork.</h2><table class="compatibility-table"><thead><tr><th>Integration</th><th>Current status</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Pi</td><td>Source prototype; fixture/runtime tests reported in project handoff</td></tr><tr><td>DeepSeek Harness</td><td>Planned adapter; no released integration claimed</td></tr><tr><td>macOS, Linux, native Windows</td><td>Launch targets; release-specific verification required</td></tr></tbody></table><p>We will list verified OS versions, architectures, and installation artifacts with each release. Native Windows is the target; WSL is outside initial scope.</p></section><section id="account"><h2>Connect a device deliberately.</h2><p>The production pairing flow starts with <code>zusal login</code>: sign in in your browser, verify the device name/code, and approve it. On a remote machine, enter the one-use device code instead.</p><p>Approve only a login you initiated. Never send account tokens or provider keys to support. Connected devices can be revoked individually from Account Settings. Device pairing alone does not start your trial.</p><p>This site includes the approval interface and backend integration source. The existing Pi launcher still needs to be connected to the production pairing/telemetry API.</p></section><section id="usage"><h2>Read the numbers.</h2><ul><li><strong>Model requests:</strong> actual execution-model calls, including tool continuations and retry attempts. Jev assessment calls are excluded.</li><li><strong>Tokens:</strong> reported input and output usage. Unavailable data stays unavailable.</li><li><strong>Estimated model cost:</strong> an estimate of external provider usage, not your Zusal subscription invoice.</li><li><strong>Timing:</strong> routing-assessment time and model-response duration are distinct.</li></ul><p>Request details and CSV exports cover 30 days. Daily totals cover 12 months. Filters use the timezone labeled in your dashboard; delayed reporting is identified separately from zero activity.</p></section><section id="privacy"><h2>Useful metadata. Clear boundaries.</h2><p>Dashboard records exclude prompts, responses, and source code. Reporting uses model identities, timestamps, tokens, estimates, status, timing, and request IDs.</p><p>Assessment is a separate data flow: compact conversation text can go to the selection backend and TypeSafe’s Jev service. Ordinary text and tool-result excerpts may contain private code; they are not automatically redacted. “Metadata-only reporting” does not mean no conversation text leaves your machine.</p><a href="/privacy">Read the privacy draft ↗</a></section><section id="support"><h2>Find your way forward.</h2><h3>Nothing appears in usage</h3><p>Check whether your CLI is connected and reporting completions. The current local selection logs do not provide provider token/cost telemetry on their own.</p><h3>A route falls back unexpectedly</h3><p>Confirm catalog/allowlist overlap, backend connection, and model availability. A successful fallback is not proof the backend selected that model.</p><h3>Ask the community</h3><p>Use public Discord channels for general help and private tickets for account/payment concerns. Share sanitized version and error codes, never credentials or private prompts.</p>{discord?<a class="button primary" href={discord} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Join Zusal Discord ↗</a>:<div class="notice">The community invite has not been configured yet. It will appear here when the server is ready.</div>}</section></div></div></div></BaseLayout>
